Line 4: The keys of "ages" are text, but this is a number.
    say the value for 3 in ages
                      ^

Line 5: I can only look up a value in a map, but this is a list of numbers.
    say the value for "Ada" in scores
                               ^^^^^^
To take an item from a list, write "item 2 of scores".

Line 6: "ages" is a map from text to numbers, so it can't hold text.
    set the value for "Ada" in ages to "old"
                                       ^^^^^
A map holds one kind of value. To keep text too, make a separate map for it.

Line 7: "ages" is a map from text to numbers, so the value after ", or" has to be a number, but this is text.
    say the value for "Ada" in ages, or "none"
                                        ^^^^^^

Line 8: I can only check whether a map has a key, but this is a list of numbers.
    if scores has 72, say "yes"
       ^^^^^^
To check whether a list holds something, use "contains".

Line 9: The keys of "ages" are text, but this is a yes/no value.
    if ages has yes, say "yes"
                ^^^

Line 10: I can only count the entries of a map, but this is a list of numbers.
    say the number of entries in scores
                                 ^^^^^^
To count the items of a list, write "the number of items in scores".

Line 11: I can only take the keys of a map, but this is a list of numbers.
    say the keys of scores
                    ^^^^^^

